The equation of the plane containing the line $\frac{x+1}{-3}=\frac{y-3}{2}=\frac{z+2}{1}$ is a $(x+1)+b (y-3)+c(z+2)=0$ where $-3 a+2 b+c=0$
This passes through $(0,7,-7) $
\begin{aligned}
& \therefore a(0+1)+b(7-3)+c(-7+2)=0 \\
& \Rightarrow a+4 b-5 c=0 \quad $\text{...(B) }$\end{aligned}

On solving equation (A)$ and (B) we geta=1, b=1, c=1 \therefore$ Required plane is
$ \begin{aligned}
& x+1+y-3+z+2=0 \\
& \Rightarrow x+y+z=0
\end{aligned} $

